        {
          "slug": "breaking",
          "name": "Breaking",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The original hip-hop dance, born at 1970s Bronx block parties: toprock, footwork, power moves and freezes.",
          "description": "Breaking (the media called it breakdancing) started at Bronx block parties in the early 1970s, where DJ Kool Herc looped the instrumental \"breaks\" of funk records and dancers — b-boys and b-girls — went off during them. It borrows from James Brown's footwork, martial arts, gymnastics and Latin dance, and it is organised around four elements: toprock (standing), go-downs and footwork (on the floor), power moves (continuous spinning) and freezes (held poses). It became an Olympic sport at Paris 2024, but at heart it is a battle-and-cypher culture.",
          "tips": [
            "Learn all four elements, not just power. Judges and cyphers value foundation, musicality and originality.",
            "Warm up wrists, shoulders and neck before every session — breaking loads joints most dances never touch."
          ],
          "related": ["toprock", "footwork", "power-moves", "freezes"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "footwork",
          "name": "Footwork",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Floor steps done on the hands and feet — also called downrock.",
          "description": "Footwork (downrock) is the dancing you do on the floor, supported by the hands, with the legs stepping, sweeping and threading around the body. The six-step is the foundation; threes, CCs, sweeps and threads are built from it. Good footwork is fast, clean and musical, and it is what connects toprock to freezes and power moves.",
          "tips": [
            "Keep the hips up and the weight over the hands, so the legs have room to move.",
            "Drill footwork slowly on both sides. Speed comes from clean positions, not from rushing."
          ],
          "related": ["six-step", "three-step", "cc", "go-down"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "power-moves",
          "name": "Power moves",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Continuous rotating moves driven by momentum — windmills, flares, headspins.",
          "description": "Power moves are the acrobatic, continuous rotations of breaking: the windmill, the flare, the headspin, the airflare and many more. They are driven by momentum and strength, and they are the part most people picture when they hear \"breakdance\". In battles they are one element among four, and they must connect musically to the rest of a round.",
          "tips": [
            "Build the strength base first: handstands, turtles and freezes make power moves safer.",
            "Learn on a smooth, clean floor, and use a beanie for anything on the head."
          ],
          "related": ["windmill", "flare", "headspin", "turtle"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "freezes",
          "name": "Freezes",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Held poses that stop the motion dead, usually on a hit in the music.",
          "description": "A freeze stops the dance in a held position — balanced on the hands, head, elbows or shoulders — often to hit a beat or end a round. Freezes range from the beginner baby freeze and chair freeze to the airchair and hollowback. They also build the balance and strength power moves need.",
          "tips": [
            "Hold each freeze for a few seconds in practice, then practise getting into it on a beat.",
            "Stack joints under the weight: wrist under elbow, elbow under the body."
          ],
          "related": ["baby-freeze", "chair-freeze", "turtle", "headstand"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "breaks",
          "name": "Breaks",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The drum-heavy instrumental sections of records that breaking is named after.",
          "description": "The break is the part of a funk or soul record where the other instruments drop out and the drums keep going. DJs like Kool Herc and Grandmaster Flash extended them by switching between two copies of a record, and that is where the dance got its name. Classic break tracks — \"Apache\", \"The Mexican\", \"It's Just Begun\", \"Give It Up or Turnit a Loose\" — are still played at jams.",
          "tips": [
            "Learn the classic breaks. Dancing to them is how you learn what the music wants."
          ],
          "related": ["breaking", "musicality"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "musicality",
          "name": "Musicality",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Hitting breaks, drum rolls and changes in the track, not just dancing through them.",
          "description": "In breaking, musicality means dancing the record: toprock that rides the groove, footwork that speeds up with the drums, freezes that land on a hit. It is one of the main judging criteria in battles.",
          "tips": [
            "Practise freezing on the last beat of a phrase until it becomes automatic."
          ],
          "related": ["breaks", "battle", "freezes"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "battle",
          "name": "Battle and cypher",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Breaking's two stages: the circle where dancers take turns, and head-to-head competition.",
          "description": "A cypher is the circle where b-boys and b-girls take turns; a battle puts dancers or crews head to head in rounds, judged on foundation, technique, musicality, creativity and character. A \"burn\" is a gesture or move aimed at the opponent. Crews like Rock Steady Crew kept the culture alive and competitive from the 1970s on.",
          "tips": [
            "A round usually goes toprock, go-down, footwork, power or tricks, and a freeze to finish."
          ],
          "related": ["breaking", "musicality", "toprock"]
        }
      ]
